{# Suggested heading for the host SKILL.md:
   ## Helper: Dispatch agent task

   Place at top level, after the `## Workflow` section and
   before `## Rules` / `## Completion`.

   Variables (must be passed by the includer):
     prefix — uppercase shell-variable prefix, e.g. "SB"
     shim   — uppercase wrapper-script variable, e.g. "LOOM";
              must resolve to the loom CLI (loom.sh) or a
              wrapper forwarding args verbatim
#}
`${{ prefix }}_{{ shim }} runtime next` yields ready agent tasks with
their `prompt_path` already rendered from the task's materialised
`input.yaml`. For each entry, dispatch via the `subagent` MCP tool
with `role: trusted` (grants file-read/write access).

The sub-agent's `prompt_template` should instruct it to `fs_read` the
`prompt_path` and follow it. The agent writes its output to the
`output_path` from the same entry (or via
`${{ prefix }}_{{ shim }} output add`).

Completing the task is a SEPARATE tool turn, never the same parallel
batch as the dispatch: wait for the sub-agent result, `fs_read` the
`output_path` and confirm it is non-empty, then call
`${{ prefix }}_{{ shim }} runtime complete "${{ prefix }}_WD" "<task-address>"`.
`complete` reads whatever output exists at call time — for a loop-body
task it falls back to the previous round — so a batched `complete`
races the sub-agent, marks the task done on stale data, and the next
`runtime next` fails on an unresolvable `@prev` reference.

Dispatch independent entries in parallel via the `subagent` `stages`
array with no `depends_on`; complete them afterwards, one `complete`
per returned result.
